#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097816 :tutorial/content "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result in a future lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "b1f46211-3766-53d0-bcc7-30bb9056b948" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2615 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097817 :tutorial/content "For a general logarithm of base $a,$ the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x =  \\log_{10} x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{log}x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({log}_{10}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{\\operatorname{ln}a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}a}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}a}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a7450e4-ec0e-5a5e-9c86-9430eb889be7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2616 :tutorial/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function with a General Base"}]}
